from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2025-june','-q001-060')
def add(n,topic,p,o,c,h,s,t,e):w.add(n,'Mathematics',topic,'Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e)
add(32,'Linear Algebra',r'''V={ax³+bx²+cx:a,b,c∈ℝ}. For f∈V define Q(f)=∫₋₁¹(f′(t))²dt. Which assertion is FALSE?''',['Q is a positive definite quadratic form on V.','Q takes every positive real value.','Q(x)=2.','For all f,g∈V, Q(f+g)=Q(f)+Q(g).'],'D',['Expand the square of f′+g′.','Test the claimed additivity using f=g=x.'],r'''Q is quadratic, and Q(f)≥0. If Q(f)=0, continuity and nonnegativity imply f′≡0, hence f is constant. V contains no nonzero constants, so f=0; Q is positive definite. Also Q(x)=∫₋₁¹1dt=2. For any r>0, Q(√(r/2)x)=r. But Q(x+x)=Q(2x)=8, while Q(x)+Q(x)=4. Thus D is false; the missing cross term is 2∫f′g′.''',r'''A real quadratic form Q comes from a symmetric bilinear form B through Q(v)=B(v,v), and satisfies Q(cv)=c²Q(v). It is positive definite if Q(v)>0 for every nonzero v. Integral expressions such as ∫(f′)² are automatically nonnegative, but their kernel must be checked: derivative zero means a constant function, so positivity depends on whether nonzero constants are allowed in the underlying space. Continuity ensures that a nonnegative integrand with zero integral vanishes everywhere. Quadratic forms are generally not additive; expansion gives Q(u+v)=Q(u)+2B(u,v)+Q(v). Positive scaling lets a nonzero positive quadratic form attain every positive value. Confusing a quadratic form with a linear functional is the central pitfall; homogeneity has degree two, not one.''',[('On V=span{x,x²}, is ∫₀¹(f′)² positive definite?','Zero integral forces f′=0, and the only constant in V is zero, so yes. For f=ax+bx² the value is a²+2ab+4b²/3.'),('For Q(a,b)=a²+b², test Q((1,0)+(1,0))=Q(1,0)+Q(1,0).','The left side is Q(2,0)=4 and the right side is 2. The cross term is 2, so additivity fails.')])
add(33,'Complex Analysis',r'''Let f:ℂ→ℂ be a polynomial. For R>0 let γ_R(t)=Re^{2πit}, 0≤t≤1. Suppose ∫₀¹|(f∘γ_R)(t)γ_R′(t)|dt→c∈ℝ as R→∞. Which is FALSE?''',[r'zf(1/z)→0 as |z|→∞.','f is constant.','c=0.','c>0.'],'D',['The displayed absolute value gives an integral of magnitudes, not a signed contour integral.','For a nonzero degree-d polynomial, its modulus on a large circle is uniformly comparable to Rᵈ.'],r'''Suppose f≠0 has degree d≥0 and leading coefficient a_d≠0. Uniformly in θ, f(Re^{iθ})/(a_dRᵈe^{idθ})→1, so for sufficiently large R, |f(Re^{iθ})|≥|a_d|Rᵈ/2. Also |γ_R′(t)|=2πR. The integral is therefore ≥π|a_d|R^{d+1}→∞, contrary to its finite limit. Hence f≡0, and c=0. This makes A,B,C true and c>0 false.''',r'''A polynomial's leading term controls its growth uniformly on large circles. If f(z)=a_dzᵈ plus lower-degree terms with a_d≠0, the sum of lower terms divided by |a_d|Rᵈ tends to zero uniformly for |z|=R. Thus its modulus is eventually bounded below by a positive multiple of Rᵈ. The parameterized circle γ_R(t)=Re^{2πit} has speed |γ_R′|=2πR, so integration of |f(γ_R)γ_R′| measures the magnitude of f integrated against arc length. It is fundamentally different from a complex contour integral ∫f(z)dz, where cancellations can yield zero for every polynomial. A finite limiting magnitude integral excludes every nonzero polynomial, including a nonzero constant. Always locate absolute-value bars precisely before applying Cauchy's theorem or asymptotic arguments.''',[('Evaluate ∫₀¹|γ_R′(t)|dt for γ_R=Re^{2πit}.','The speed is constant 2πR, so the integral is 2πR, the circumference; it diverges as R→∞.'),('Compare ∮_|z|=R z²dz with ∮_|z|=R |z²||dz|.','The analytic integral is zero because z² has primitive z³/3. The magnitude integral equals R² times circumference 2πR, hence 2πR³.')])
add(34,'Complex Analysis',r'''Let X be the image of [0,1] under f(z)=(z−i)/(z+i). Which is true?''',['X is the line segment joining −1 and −i.',r'X={e^{iθ}:θ∈[π,3π/2]}.','X is the line segment joining −1 and 1.',r'X={e^{iθ}:θ∈[−π/2,π]}.'],'B',['For real x, numerator and denominator have equal modulus.','Find the endpoint images and the signs of real and imaginary parts for 0<x<1.'],r'''For real x, f(x)=[(x−i)²]/(x²+1)=(x²−1)/(x²+1)−i·2x/(x²+1), with |f(x)|=1. It sends 0 to −1 and 1 to −i, with both real and imaginary parts negative in between. More explicitly f(x)=e^{i(π+2 arctan x)}. As x rises from 0 to 1, that angle rises continuously from π to 3π/2. Thus X is exactly the lower-left quarter of the unit circle, option B.''',r'''A Möbius transformation (az+b)/(cz+d), with ad−bc≠0, is a bijection of the extended complex plane and maps generalized circles to generalized circles. A generalized circle is either a circle or a line, with the point at infinity included for lines. For a real variable x, the Cayley transformation (x−i)/(x+i) has modulus one because x−i and x+i are complex conjugates. To identify an interval image, endpoint images alone are insufficient: a circle has two connecting arcs, and one must determine the interior location or a continuous argument. Multiplying numerator and denominator by the conjugate separates real and imaginary parts. A parameter such as arctan x then identifies the traversed angular interval. Avoid replacing a curved arc with the straight chord connecting its endpoints.''',[('Find the image of [0,∞) under (x−i)/(x+i).','The angle π+2arctan x ranges from π toward 2π, excluding 2π for finite x. Thus the image is the lower semicircle from −1 inclusive to 1 exclusive.'),('Compute the image of x=√3 under the same transformation.','The angle is π+2π/3=5π/3. Thus the image is 1/2−i√3/2, also obtained from real part (3−1)/4 and imaginary part −2√3/4.')])
add(35,'Complex Analysis',r'Which assertion is true? There exists an entire function f such that, for every positive integer n:',[r'f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=n!/nⁿ.',r'f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=n!nⁿ.',r'f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=(n−1)!.',r'f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=n!n.'],'A',['Taylor coefficients are derivatives divided by n!.','An entire function requires infinite radius of convergence; examine the nth roots of the coefficients.'],r'''For A the Taylor coefficient is a_n=1/nⁿ, so |a_n|^{1/n}=1/n→0. The series a₀+Σₙ≥₁zⁿ/nⁿ has infinite radius and defines the required entire function. For B, coefficients nⁿ have nth roots n→∞, giving radius zero. For C, coefficients 1/n have nth roots tending to 1, giving radius 1. For D, coefficients n also have nth roots tending to 1, giving radius 1. Uniqueness of Taylor coefficients rules out alternative entire functions satisfying those latter derivative data.''',r'''An entire function is holomorphic on all of ℂ. Its Taylor expansion at zero is f(z)=Σa_nzⁿ with coefficients a_n=f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)/n!, and the series converges throughout the plane. The Cauchy–Hadamard formula states 1/R=limsup|a_n|^{1/n}, with R=∞ when that limsup is zero and R=0 when it is infinite. Derivative prescriptions therefore determine all nonconstant Taylor coefficients and leave only the constant term free. A power series with infinite radius constructs the desired entire function, whereas a finite radius proves that no entire function can have those coefficients, by uniqueness of analytic expansions. The factorial division is essential: the growth of the derivatives themselves cannot be used as though they were the power-series coefficients.''',[('Can an entire function satisfy f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=1 for every n≥1?','Coefficients are 1/n!, whose nth roots tend to zero. The function eᶻ−1, with arbitrary added constant, satisfies the requirement.'),('Can an entire function satisfy f⁽ⁿ⁾(0)=n!2ⁿ?','Coefficients 2ⁿ have nth-root limit 2, so radius is 1/2. They produce 1/(1−2z) near zero, which has a pole; no entire function exists.')])
add(36,'Complex Analysis',r'''Let f be entire and f(ℂ)⊂{x+iy:y=x+1}. Which is true?''',[r'|f(z)|→∞ as |z|→∞.',r'f(z)/z→0 as |z|→∞.',r'zf(z)→0 as |z|→∞.',r'f(z)→0 as |z|→∞.'],'B',['A line is not an open subset of the complex plane.','Apply the open mapping theorem to show f is constant.'],r'''If f were nonconstant, the open mapping theorem would make f(ℂ) open in ℂ. A subset of the line y=x+1 cannot contain a nonempty open disk, so f is constant, say f≡c on that line. Then c/z→0 as |z|→∞. The line does not contain zero, so c≠0: |f| stays finite and positive, |zf| diverges, and f does not tend to zero. Thus only B is true.''',r'''The open mapping theorem says that a nonconstant holomorphic function on a connected open domain sends open sets to open sets. In particular, a nonconstant entire function has an image open in ℂ. A line, circle, or other set with empty interior cannot contain such an image; holomorphic functions whose values are constrained to these sets must therefore be constant. This argument does not require boundedness, unlike Liouville's theorem. One can also derive constancy from the Cauchy–Riemann equations when real and imaginary parts satisfy a linear relation. Once constancy is established, evaluate each claimed asymptotic behavior for that constant, including whether zero belongs to the prescribed image set. A constant function need not be zero, and multiplying it by z and dividing it by z have very different limits.''',[('If an entire function has real values everywhere, prove it is constant.','The real axis has empty interior, whereas a nonconstant holomorphic image is open. Therefore the function is constant.'),('An entire function has values on |w|=3. Find the limit of f(z)/z.','The circular image restriction forces f≡c with |c|=3. Hence |f(z)/z|=3/|z|→0.')])
add(37,'Number Theory',r'Which statement is true?',[r'p∤1+(p−1)! for some odd prime p.',r'p|(1234)^{p−1}−1 for all primes p>700.',r'There exist a∈ℤ and a prime p>11 with p∤aᵖ−a.',r'p∤(p²)!/(p!)² for some odd prime p.'],'B',['Recall Wilson’s theorem and both forms of Fermat’s little theorem.','Factor 1234, and count the p-factors in the factorial quotient.'],r'''Wilson gives (p−1)!≡−1 mod p for every prime, so A is false. Since 1234=2·617, every prime p>700 is coprime to 1234; Fermat gives 1234^{p−1}≡1 mod p, proving B. Fermat also gives aᵖ≡a for all integers a, disproving C. For D use Legendre: v_p((p²)!)=p+1 and v_p((p!)²)=2. The quotient has p-adic valuation p−1>0, so p divides it for every odd prime. Thus D is false.''',r'''Fermat's little theorem states aᵖ≡a modulo a prime p for every integer a. When p does not divide a, cancelling a yields a^{p−1}≡1. Wilson's theorem characterizes primes by (p−1)!≡−1 mod p. Legendre's formula counts prime factors in factorials: v_p(n!)=Σₖ≥₁⌊n/pᵏ⌋. The valuation of a product is the sum of valuations, and of an integer quotient the difference. A positive valuation means divisibility by p. Before applying Fermat's exponent p−1 form, verify coprimality by factoring the base or ruling out all its prime factors. For factorial quotients, ordinary cancellation is often inefficient; valuation counts handle repeated powers systematically. A counterexample to a universally quantified nondivisibility statement must respect all stated prime restrictions.''',[('Show 7 divides 10⁶−1.','Since 7 does not divide 10 and 7 is prime, Fermat gives 10⁶≡1 mod 7, proving the divisibility.'),('Find v₃(12!).','Legendre gives ⌊12/3⌋+⌊12/9⌋=4+1=5, so 3⁵ divides 12! and 3⁶ does not.')])
add(38,'Abstract Algebra',r'''Let A be a subring of ℚ such that for every nonzero rational r, either r∈A or 1/r∈A. Which statement is FALSE?''',[r'{a∈A:1/a∉A}∪{0} is an additive subgroup of ℚ.',r'A has at most one maximal ideal.',r'If A≠ℚ, A has infinitely many prime ideals.',r'For any nonzero a,b∈A, a divides b or b divides a in A.'],'C',['The hypothesis makes A a valuation ring: compare a/b and b/a.','Use the localization ℤ_(2), whose denominators are odd, as a counterexample to the prime-ideal claim.'],r'''The hypothesis applied to 1 implies 1∈A. For a,b≠0 in A, a/b or b/a lies in A, hence one divides the other, proving D. Let m be zero together with the nonunits. If a,b are nonzero nonunits, suppose b/a∈A (the other case is symmetric); then a+b=a(1+b/a) cannot be a unit, since that would make a a unit. Negation and multiplication by ring elements preserve nonunits, so m is an ideal and in particular an additive subgroup. Every proper ideal contains only nonunits, and A/m is a field; thus m is the unique maximal ideal unless A is already a field (then m={0} is maximal). For C take A=ℤ_(2)={m/n:n odd}. It has the required reciprocal property, is not ℚ, and its only prime ideals are (0) and 2A. Indeed localization retains precisely prime ideals of ℤ disjoint from the odd integers, namely (0),(2). Thus C is false.''',r'''A valuation ring of a field K is a subring A in which every nonzero r∈K has r or its reciprocal in A. This condition implies comparability of divisibility for any two nonzero elements of A. The nonunits form an ideal: comparability factors a sum of nonunits by one of them, and a product containing a nonunit cannot become invertible. Since every element outside this ideal is a unit, it is the unique maximal ideal, so valuation rings are local. The localization ℤ_(p) consists of fractions with denominator not divisible by p and is a discrete valuation ring. Its prime ideals are (0) and pℤ_(p), obtained from prime ideals of ℤ avoiding the multiplicative set. A proper subring of a field need not have infinitely many prime ideals; locality and dimension are separate properties.''',[('Why is ℤ_(3) a valuation ring of ℚ?','Write a rational in lowest terms m/n. If 3∤n it lies in ℤ_(3). If 3|n, coprimality gives 3∤m, so its reciprocal n/m lies there.'),('Identify units and maximal ideal of ℤ_(5).','A fraction m/n with 5∤n is invertible precisely when 5∤m. Nonunits have numerator divisible by 5 and form 5ℤ_(5), the unique maximal ideal.')])
add(39,'Abstract Algebra','Which statement is true?',[r'The ideal 2ℤ[i] is maximal in ℤ[i].',r'The ideal Xℂ[X,Y] is maximal in ℂ[X,Y].',r'Polynomials in ℂ[X] whose coefficients sum to 0 form a maximal ideal.',r'The ideal (√2−1)ℤ[√2] is maximal in ℤ[√2].'],'C',['An ideal is maximal exactly when the quotient is a field.','Summing coefficients is evaluation at X=1; check whether √2−1 is a unit.'],r'''Evaluation ev₁:ℂ[X]→ℂ is onto, and its kernel consists exactly of polynomials whose coefficient sum is zero, namely (X−1). The quotient is ℂ, a field, so C is true. A is false: in ℤ[i]/(2), i+1 is a nonzero nilpotent, since (i+1)²=2i=0, so the quotient is not a field. B has quotient ℂ[Y], not a field. D is the whole ring because (√2−1)(√2+1)=1; a maximal ideal must be proper.''',r'''For a commutative ring with identity, a proper ideal I is maximal if and only if R/I is a field, and prime if and only if the quotient is an integral domain. A surjective ring homomorphism to a field therefore has maximal kernel by the first isomorphism theorem. Evaluation at a point a maps K[X] onto the field K and has kernel (X−a), giving a standard maximal ideal. In several variables, removing only one variable usually leaves a polynomial ring, which is not a field. An ideal generated by a unit equals the entire ring and cannot be maximal. Nonzero nilpotents in a quotient disprove that it is a domain or field. Check properness and the actual quotient rather than assuming that a familiar prime integer remains prime in a larger ring.''',[('Is (X−2) maximal in ℝ[X]?','Evaluation at 2 has kernel (X−2) and image ℝ. Thus ℝ[X]/(X−2)≅ℝ is a field, so the ideal is maximal.'),('Is (X,Y) maximal in ℂ[X,Y]?','Evaluation at (0,0) maps onto ℂ and has kernel (X,Y). The quotient is ℂ, hence the ideal is maximal.')])
add(40,'Topology',r'S={1,2,3,4,5} has topology τ={∅,{1},S}. How many homeomorphisms of S onto itself exist?',['25','120','24','6'],'C',['A homeomorphism must preserve the unique nonempty proper open set.','After fixing 1, the remaining four points may be freely permuted.'],'For a bijection h:S→S, continuity requires h⁻¹({1}) to be open. This preimage is a singleton, and the only open singleton is {1}, so h(1)=1. Conversely every bijection fixing 1 preserves ∅,{1},S and its inverse does too, hence is a homeomorphism. There are 4!=24 permutations of the other four points.',r'''A topology on a set specifies the open subsets and must contain the empty set and whole space, be closed under arbitrary unions, and under finite intersections. A function is continuous when inverse images of open sets are open. A homeomorphism is a bijection whose map and inverse are continuous; it preserves the full topology, not just the underlying cardinality. On a finite space, identifying distinguished open sets can dramatically restrict allowable permutations. For the topology consisting only of ∅, one singled-out singleton, and the whole set, that unique open singleton must be fixed. All other points have identical topological roles and may be permuted freely. A bijection alone need not be a homeomorphism, and counting all n! permutations ignores any distinguished open subsets.''',[('On a four-point set with topology {∅,{a},S}, count self-homeomorphisms.','They must fix a; every permutation of the remaining three points works. Thus the count is 3!=6.'),('Count self-homeomorphisms of a three-point discrete space.','Every subset is open, so every bijection and its inverse are continuous. All 3!=6 permutations are homeomorphisms.')])
add(41,'Ordinary Differential Equations',r'''Given that φ(x)=x solves y″−(2/x²+1/x)(xy′−y)=0 for 0<x<∞, what is the general solution?''',[r'(a+be^{−2x})x, a,b∈ℝ.',r'(a+be^{2x})x, a,b∈ℝ.',r'aeˣ+bx, a,b∈ℝ.',r'(a+beˣ)x, a,b∈ℝ.'],'D',['Use the known nonzero solution by setting y=xv.','Compute xy′−y and y″; cancellations reduce the equation to v″−v′=0.'],r'''Set y=xv. Then y′=v+xv′, y″=2v′+xv″, and xy′−y=x²v′. The ODE becomes 2v′+xv″−(2+x)v′=x(v″−v′)=0. Since x>0, v″−v′=0, so v′=Ceˣ and v=a+beˣ. Thus y=x(a+beˣ). The two solutions x and xeˣ are independent because their Wronskian is x²eˣ≠0 on the domain, confirming that this is the full two-parameter solution.''',r'''A homogeneous linear second-order differential equation y″+P(x)y′+Q(x)y=0 has a two-dimensional solution space on an interval where its coefficients are continuous. If a nonzero solution y₁ is known, reduction of order sets y=y₁v, and the term proportional to v disappears because y₁ already satisfies the equation. The resulting equation involves v′ and v″ and can be reduced to a first-order equation for v′. Dividing by y₁ requires an interval where it does not vanish. Two solutions form a fundamental system if their Wronskian y₁y₂′−y₁′y₂ is nonzero at a point; then every solution is their linear combination. Domain restrictions matter when coefficients have singularities such as 1/x or 1/x². Carefully expand composite expressions such as xy′−y before substituting.''',[('Solve y″−(2/x)y′+(2/x²)y=0 on x>0 using y₁=x.','Set y=xv. Substitution gives xv″=0, hence v=a+bx. Thus y=ax+bx²; their Wronskian x² is nonzero.'),('Solve v″−2v′=0.','Let u=v′. Then u′−2u=0 gives u=Ce^{2x}; integrating yields v=A+Be^{2x}.')])
print(w.path,len(w.packs))
